   context="The 1850s, the new English Orientalism and the study of Persian (Edward Cowell and the Bodleian manuscript), the Victorian crisis of faith, and the aesthetic movement's cult of melancholy pleasure.",
   sig="The Rubáiyát is the most popular English poem of the later nineteenth century and the founding text of the vogue for Persian literature; its celebration of the present moment and its scepticism made it the philosophical poem of the 1890s aesthetic movement, and its influence on the modern English quatrain and on the decoration (Rossetti, Morris, and the Kelmscott circle) is direct.",
